---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import BreathingExerciser from '../../components/BreathingExerciser.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title="Breathing Exerciser | Soare Robert Daniel" description="Beginner-friendly breathing tool with seven evidence-informed techniques including Belly Breathing, Box Breathing, 4-7-8, Cyclic Sighing, and more." />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section>
				<h1>Breathing Exerciser</h1>
				<div class="divider"></div>

				<p class="intro">
					Seven simple, evidence-informed breathing techniques you can try right now — no experience needed. Pick one based on what you want (calm, sleep, focus), press Start, and just follow the growing circle and the words on screen. The circle gets bigger as you breathe in and smaller as you breathe out.
				</p>

				<BreathingExerciser />

				<div class="divider" style="margin: 3rem 0 2rem;"></div>

				<h2>Technique Guide</h2>
				<p class="section-subtitle">A quick reference: what each method is, how to do it, and when to use it.</p>

				<div class="technique-detail">
					<div class="td-head">
						<h3>Belly Breathing</h3>
						<span class="tag">Start here</span>
					</div>
					<div class="timing">In 4s → Out 6s <span class="cycle">10s cycle</span></div>
					<p class="td-desc">Slow, low breathing into your belly instead of your chest. It is the foundation that every other technique builds on — if you only learn one, learn this.</p>
					<div class="td-cols">
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>How to do it</h4>
							<ol>
								<li>Rest one hand on your chest and one on your belly.</li>
								<li>Breathe in through your nose so your belly rises and your chest stays still.</li>
								<li>Breathe out gently through pursed lips and feel your belly fall.</li>
							</ol>
						</div>
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>Why it helps</h4>
							<p>The diaphragm does most of the work of quiet breathing, but stress nudges us into shallow chest breathing. Breathing low and slow eases tension and signals your body to calm down. Recommended by <strong>Cleveland Clinic</strong> and <strong>Harvard Health</strong> as the first breathing exercise to learn.</p>
							<p class="best"><strong>Best for:</strong> everyone — the prerequisite for the other techniques.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="technique-detail">
					<div class="td-head">
						<h3>Cyclic Sighing</h3>
						<span class="tag">Stress relief</span>
					</div>
					<div class="timing">In 3s → Top-up 1s → Out 6s <span class="cycle">10s cycle</span></div>
					<p class="td-desc">Two inhales (a normal breath plus a short "top-up") followed by one long, slow exhale. The fastest way to lift your mood and settle a stressful moment.</p>
					<div class="td-cols">
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>How to do it</h4>
							<ol>
								<li>Breathe in through your nose to fill most of your lungs.</li>
								<li>Take a second short sip of air on top to fill them completely.</li>
								<li>Let a long, slow breath out through your mouth until empty.</li>
							</ol>
						</div>
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>Why it helps</h4>
							<p>The double inhale reopens collapsed air sacs in the lungs, and the long exhale activates the calming branch of your nervous system. A <strong>2023 Stanford study</strong> (Cell Reports Medicine) found five minutes a day improved mood more than meditation — the strongest evidence of any technique here.</p>
							<p class="best"><strong>Best for:</strong> instant relief (1–3 breaths) or a daily 5-minute practice.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="technique-detail">
					<div class="td-head">
						<h3>Tactical Breathing</h3>
						<span class="tag">Stress relief</span>
					</div>
					<div class="timing">In 4s → Out 4s <span class="cycle">8s cycle</span></div>
					<p class="td-desc">The simplest pattern here: just breathe in and out at an even, slow pace. No holds to remember, so it works even when you are too stressed to think.</p>
					<div class="td-cols">
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>How to do it</h4>
							<ol>
								<li>Breathe in slowly through your nose for a count of four.</li>
								<li>Breathe out slowly through your nose for a count of four.</li>
								<li>Keep the rhythm even and let your shoulders relax.</li>
							</ol>
						</div>
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>Why it helps</h4>
							<p>Slowing your breath and counting redirects attention away from the stressor and lowers your breathing rate. Taught to military and police (<strong>Lt. Col. Dave Grossman, <em>On Combat</em></strong>) precisely because it is easy to follow under pressure.</p>
							<p class="best"><strong>Best for:</strong> quick calming in the moment — no holds required.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="technique-detail">
					<div class="td-head">
						<h3>4-7-8 Breath</h3>
						<span class="tag">Sleep</span>
					</div>
					<div class="timing">In 4s → Hold 7s → Out 8s <span class="cycle">19s cycle</span></div>
					<p class="td-desc">A short inhale, a gentle hold, and a long exhale. The extended out-breath is the key — it nudges your body toward rest.</p>
					<div class="td-cols">
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>How to do it</h4>
							<ol>
								<li>Breathe in quietly through your nose for a count of four.</li>
								<li>Hold your breath gently for a count of seven.</li>
								<li>Breathe out fully through your mouth for a count of eight. Repeat up to four times.</li>
							</ol>
						</div>
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>Why it helps</h4>
							<p>The long exhale activates the calming (parasympathetic) nervous system, slowing heart rate. Developed by <strong>Dr. Andrew Weil</strong>; <strong>Cleveland Clinic</strong> suggests it before bed to help you relax and fall asleep.</p>
							<p class="best"><strong>Best for:</strong> falling asleep and easing anxiety spikes.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="technique-detail">
					<div class="td-head">
						<h3>Box Breathing</h3>
						<span class="tag">Focus</span>
					</div>
					<div class="timing">In 4s → Hold 4s → Out 4s → Hold 4s <span class="cycle">16s cycle</span></div>
					<p class="td-desc">Four equal phases that form a "box". The steady, predictable rhythm helps you hold your focus under pressure.</p>
					<div class="td-cols">
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>How to do it</h4>
							<ol>
								<li>Breathe in through your nose for a count of four.</li>
								<li>Hold your breath gently for a count of four.</li>
								<li>Breathe out for four, then hold empty for four. Repeat.</li>
							</ol>
						</div>
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>Why it helps</h4>
							<p>Equal phases create a calming rhythm and the holds build tolerance to the urge to gasp. Used by the <strong>US Navy SEALs</strong> and first responders, and covered by <strong>WebMD</strong>, <strong>Verywell Health</strong>, and <strong>Cleveland Clinic</strong>.</p>
							<p class="best"><strong>Best for:</strong> focus and composure before a stressful task.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="technique-detail">
					<div class="td-head">
						<h3>Coherent Breathing</h3>
						<span class="tag">Gentle / balance</span>
					</div>
					<div class="timing">In 5s → Out 5s <span class="cycle">10s cycle</span></div>
					<p class="td-desc">Slow, even breaths at about five per minute. The gentlest option — great for everyday balance and building long-term resilience.</p>
					<div class="td-cols">
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>How to do it</h4>
							<ol>
								<li>Breathe in smoothly through your nose for a count of five.</li>
								<li>Breathe out smoothly for a count of five.</li>
								<li>Keep it continuous — about six breaths a minute.</li>
							</ol>
						</div>
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>Why it helps</h4>
							<p>Breathing around five times a minute lines up with your heart's natural rhythm and maximises <strong>heart-rate variability</strong>, a strong marker of calm and stress resilience, according to resonance-breathing research.</p>
							<p class="best"><strong>Best for:</strong> daily regulation and general well-being.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="technique-detail">
					<div class="td-head">
						<h3>Buteyko Breathing</h3>
						<span class="tag">Gentle / balance</span>
					</div>
					<div class="timing">Small in 2s → Easy out 3s → Pause 4s <span class="cycle">~9s cycle</span></div>
					<p class="td-desc">Lighter, quieter nasal breathing with a comfortable pause after the exhale. The idea is to breathe a little less, not more.</p>
					<div class="td-cols">
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>How to do it</h4>
							<ol>
								<li>Breathe in gently and quietly through your nose — smaller than usual.</li>
								<li>Let an easy, relaxed breath out through your nose.</li>
								<li>Pause comfortably until you feel a mild urge to breathe, then repeat.</li>
							</ol>
						</div>
						<div class="detail-col">
							<h4>Why it helps</h4>
							<p>Developed by <strong>Dr. Konstantin Buteyko</strong>, the method trains lighter nasal breathing and CO₂ tolerance. Its strongest evidence is as a <strong>support for asthma symptoms</strong> (a Cochrane review found reduced symptoms and reliever use); evidence for other uses is more limited.</p>
							<p class="best"><strong>Best for:</strong> building a nasal-breathing habit; asthma support.</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="notice">
					<strong>Safety note:</strong> These exercises are generally safe, but if you feel dizzy, tingly, or uncomfortable, stop and resume later with smaller, gentler breaths. Keep all breath holds and pauses comfortable — never force them. If you are pregnant, have a heart or lung condition, or have asthma, keep breaths light and check with your clinician first.
				</div>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	h1 {
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
	}

	.intro {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		max-width: 640px;
	}

	.section-subtitle {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	.technique-detail {
		background: var(--bg-card);
		border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
		border-radius: 12px;
		padding: 1.5rem;
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
	}

	.td-head {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
	}

	.td-head h3 {
		font-size: 1.3rem;
		margin: 0;
	}

	.tag {
		display: inline-block;
		padding: 0.15rem 0.6rem;
		background: rgba(0, 240, 255, 0.08);
		border: 1px solid rgba(0, 240, 255, 0.2);
		border-radius: 999px;
		font-size: 0.7rem;
		font-weight: 500;
		color: var(--accent);
		letter-spacing: 0.05em;
	}

	.timing {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
		padding-bottom: 0.9rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
	}

	.cycle {
		margin-left: 0.5rem;
		color: var(--accent);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.td-desc {
		font-size: 0.92rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
	}

	.td-cols {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 1.5rem;
	}

	.detail-col h4 {
		font-size: 0.72rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.12em;
		color: var(--accent);
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
	}

	.detail-col ol {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.1rem;
	}

	.detail-col li {
		font-size: 0.86rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
		margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
	}

	.detail-col p {
		font-size: 0.86rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
		margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
	}

	.detail-col .best {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.notice {
		margin-top: 2rem;
		padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
		background: var(--bg-card);
		border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
		border-radius: 8px;
		font-size: 0.82rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		line-height: 1.6;
	}

	.notice strong {
		color: var(--accent);
	}

	@media (max-width: 600px) {
		.td-cols {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			gap: 1rem;
		}

		.technique-detail {
			padding: 1rem;
		}
	}
</style>
